\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Model~B regime exemplars: price, interior state fractions, and suppressed orders for a crash-prone run (left, run 9652) and an oscillatory run (right, run 5916). The crash-prone exemplar is the dramatic tail of its regime---a bubble-and-collapse trajectory ($100 \to 266 \to 83$)---shown because it makes the mechanism visible; the \emph  {typical} crash-prone run is a liquidity freeze in which the grid locks near a Sell fraction of $0.88$ while the price barely moves. ``Crash-prone'' throughout denotes majority-sell lock-in (Sell fraction $\ge 0.5$ on at least 1\% of steps), not a price discontinuity.}}{15}{figure.caption.50}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mb-exemplars}{{5}{15}{Model~B regime exemplars: price, interior state fractions, and suppressed orders for a crash-prone run (left, run 9652) and an oscillatory run (right, run 5916). The crash-prone exemplar is the dramatic tail of its regime---a bubble-and-collapse trajectory ($100 \to 266 \to 83$)---shown because it makes the mechanism visible; the \emph {typical} crash-prone run is a liquidity freeze in which the grid locks near a Sell fraction of $0.88$ while the price barely moves. ``Crash-prone'' throughout denotes majority-sell lock-in (Sell fraction $\ge 0.5$ on at least 1\% of steps), not a price discontinuity}{figure.caption.50}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Model~B seed sweep: largest-sell-cluster trajectories per seed. (Top row) For each of the three budget configurations---stable ($f = 0.10$, cash $1{,}000$), freeze ($f = 0.20$, cash $500$), and collapse ($f = 0.20$, cash $5{,}000$)---the largest sell cluster, expressed as a fraction of the grid, is drawn step by step for every one of $20$ disorder seeds. (Bottom left) Per-seed ratio of the largest cluster to the total sell population at its peak: the stable configuration sits at a median ratio of $0.022$---dispersed fluctuations with no dominant domain---while the freeze and collapse configurations concentrate into a single growing domain at median ratios of $0.26$ and $0.44$; the stable band shows zero overlap against either above-threshold configuration, the signature of a spatial nucleus above threshold and its absence below. (Bottom right) Final largest-cluster fraction of the grid at the end of the run. The time-shifted-sigmoid fan within each above-threshold configuration reflects stochastic nucleation waiting times rather than a spread of eventual outcomes.}}{16}{figure.caption.52}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mb-seedsweep}{{6}{16}{Model~B seed sweep: largest-sell-cluster trajectories per seed. (Top row) For each of the three budget configurations---stable ($f = 0.10$, cash $1{,}000$), freeze ($f = 0.20$, cash $500$), and collapse ($f = 0.20$, cash $5{,}000$)---the largest sell cluster, expressed as a fraction of the grid, is drawn step by step for every one of $20$ disorder seeds. (Bottom left) Per-seed ratio of the largest cluster to the total sell population at its peak: the stable configuration sits at a median ratio of $0.022$---dispersed fluctuations with no dominant domain---while the freeze and collapse configurations concentrate into a single growing domain at median ratios of $0.26$ and $0.44$; the stable band shows zero overlap against either above-threshold configuration, the signature of a spatial nucleus above threshold and its absence below. (Bottom right) Final largest-cluster fraction of the grid at the end of the run. The time-shifted-sigmoid fan within each above-threshold configuration reflects stochastic nucleation waiting times rather than a spread of eventual outcomes}{figure.caption.52}{}}
